add("saskatchewan-bill-80-unauthorised-gambling-advertising-ban-2026",
"Saskatchewan Bill 80: Unauthorised Gambling Ad Ban","Regulatory",
"Saskatchewan's Bill 80 would bar running or advertising unauthorised lottery schemes. What offshore operators and affiliates should watch.",
["canada"],["dickinson-wright-pllc","greenberg-traurig-llp","fox-rothschild-llp"],"2026-11-24T07:30:00Z",[
"Saskatchewan has become the latest Canadian province to move against unregulated online gambling. Bill 80, The Alcohol and Gaming Regulation Amendment Act, 2026, received first reading in the Legislative Assembly on 19 November 2026. According to reports of the bill, it would add a new section 143.1 to the province's Alcohol and Gaming Regulation Act, prohibiting any person from conducting, or holding themselves out as authorised to conduct, a lottery scheme that has not been authorised. Subject to regulations that have not yet been made, the bill would also prohibit publishing or advertising such a scheme. The government expects the bill to pass in spring 2027.",
"The constitutional backdrop explains why the drafting matters. Under Part VII of the Criminal Code, and section 207 in particular, lottery schemes, which include casino games offered online, are lawful only when conducted and managed by a province or licensed under provincial authority. Saskatchewan's regulated online offer is run through a provincially authorised channel rather than an open licensing market like Ontario's. Offshore sites that accept Saskatchewan residents therefore sit outside the provincial framework, but provinces have long found it hard to act against operators with no assets or presence in Canada. A provincial offence aimed at operation and promotion gives the regulator a local hook that does not depend on federal criminal enforcement.",
"The advertising prohibition is the most commercially significant element, and also the least developed. Reports indicate that the bill does not set new fine amounts, and its practical scope will depend on regulations that define what counts as publishing or advertising. Three questions remain open: whether the definition will reach affiliates, streamers and media publishers as well as operators; whether out-of-province online operators that never set foot in Saskatchewan will be expressly covered; and whether specific penalties will be added in later regulations or amendments. Until those points are settled, the bill signals intent more than it creates immediately enforceable liability.",
"Bill 80 also contains player-protection measures. The minimum age is reported as 19 for casinos, slot machines, video lottery terminals and online gambling, and 18 for Western Canada Lottery Corporation lottery tickets. The Saskatchewan Liquor and Gaming Authority (SLGA) would be able to use minors as test shoppers to check that retailers and venues are verifying age. Test purchasing is a familiar tool in the UK and elsewhere, and its introduction suggests SLGA intends to move from paper compliance towards active verification of age controls.",
"Warren Kaeding, the minister responsible for SLGA, has described the bill as an attempt to balance social responsibility against other considerations. That framing matters. Saskatchewan has not proposed a competitive iGaming market on the Ontario model, and Bill 80 does not create one. Its effect is to strengthen the position of authorised provincial channels by making unauthorised offers, and potentially their promotion, a distinct provincial offence.",
"Bill 80 fits a wider Canadian pattern. Provinces including Ontario and Alberta have moved to regulated models with registration requirements for operators and suppliers, and Alberta is now preparing for a regulated open market. At federal level, Senate Bill S-211 has proposed a national framework on sports betting advertising. Provinces without open markets are increasingly using advertising and promotion controls as their main tool against grey-market operators. For businesses active across Canada, the result is a patchwork in which an advertisement lawful in Ontario may be unlawful when it reaches Saskatchewan residents.",
"Operators, affiliates and media partners serving Saskatchewan should take three steps now. First, map current exposure: identify any marketing, sponsorship or affiliate traffic directed at Saskatchewan residents. Second, follow the bill through committee and watch for the advertising regulations, which will decide whether liability extends beyond operators. Third, review geotargeting and contractual controls so that Saskatchewan can be excluded quickly if the bill passes in its current form. Canadian gaming counsel can advise on how Bill 80 interacts with the Criminal Code and with regimes in other provinces."])

add("ukgc-ian-angus-licensing-authorities-inspections-aim-to-permit-2026",
"UK Licensing Authorities: Inspections Up 29%, Aim to Permit","Licensing",
"UKGC policy director Ian Angus reports a 29% rise in council gambling inspections as impact assessments and an aim to permit review loom.",
["united-kingdom"],["poppleston-allen","harris-hagan","woods-whur","joelson-llp"],"2026-11-24T08:45:00Z",[
"Local licensing in Great Britain is getting more attention from regulators. In a speech on 19 November 2026 to the Institute of Licensing National Training Conference, Ian Angus, the Gambling Commission's Director of Policy, said council gambling inspections rose 29% in 2025/26, the year to 31 March 2026, a rise he called unprecedented. Over the same period the number of licensed gambling premises fell 2% to 8,081. For land-based operators, the message is that a shrinking estate faces more local scrutiny, not less.",
"The figures come from the Commission's Licensing Authority Statistics for 2025 to 2026. They point to a change in how licensing authorities are using their powers under the Gambling Act 2005. Councils are responsible for premises licences, permits and local compliance, while the Commission regulates operating and personal licences. Angus said the Commission has handled more than 150 compliance queries from councils in the current financial year. He also gave examples of joint enforcement work in Manchester, Doncaster, Birmingham, Bristol and Sheffield, including operations that seized illegal gaming machines and closed an illegal casino.",
"For applicants and existing licence holders, the more significant developments are structural. Angus said Gambling Impact Assessments come into force in November 2026. Operators should expect licensing authorities to give more weight to evidence of local risk when deciding applications and variations, and should expect local area risk assessments, statements of principles and objection material to be tested more rigorously. Applications prepared on the assumption that a premises licence will be granted almost automatically will be increasingly exposed.",
"That assumption rests on section 153 of the Gambling Act 2005, the so-called aim to permit duty. It requires licensing authorities to aim to permit the use of premises for gambling in so far as they think it is in accordance with the relevant codes and guidance, reasonably consistent with the licensing objectives and in accordance with their statement of principles. Angus confirmed that ministers intend to consult on revoking the duty, although no date has been set. Removing it would give councils far more discretion to refuse premises licences on the basis of local concerns, and would change the balance of power in contested applications.",
"The Commission's own strategy is also changing. Its next Corporate Strategy is due to take effect in April 2027 and is still being drafted, with five priority areas not yet finalised and no published targets or budgets. Angus indicated that illegal gambling and anti-money laundering supervision are likely to lead, ahead of the UK's next FATF mutual evaluation. The Commission is using the first £7 million of a £26 million, three-year government commitment to tackle illegal gambling, and some of that work depends on cooperation with local authorities and police.",
"There are product-specific changes for operators too. From 30 June 2027, new Category B gaming machines must offer better access to gambling-management tools and display session length and net win or loss. The Commission dropped its earlier plan to apply these requirements to existing machines. Operators should build the requirements into procurement and supplier contracts for machines bought from 2027, and expect licensing officers to ask about them during inspections.",
"Land-based operators should prepare now. Review local area risk assessments for every premises, especially in areas where councils have stepped up inspections. Make sure staff training, test purchasing results and incident logs are ready for inspection. Engage early with licensing authorities that are revising their statements of principles. Contested applications, reviews and any consultation on the aim to permit duty are areas where specialist licensing counsel can make a material difference to the outcome."])

add("ksa-circus-zebet-award-betting-markets-coach-of-the-year-brownlow-2026",
"KSA Orders Circus and ZEbet to Drop Award Betting","Compliance",
"The Dutch KSA has ordered Circus and ZEbet to remove bets on the NBA Coach of the Year and AFL Brownlow Medal. Why award markets breach licences.",
["netherlands","belgium"],["kalff-katz-and-franssen","stibbe","akd-benelux-lawyers","franssen-tolboom"],"2026-11-24T10:00:00Z",[
"The Netherlands Gambling Authority, the Kansspelautoriteit (KSA), has again reminded the Dutch market that a sports betting licence does not cover every market a trading team can price. In a notice dated 19 November 2026, the KSA said it had contacted and ordered two licensees to stop offering prohibited bets. Circus, part of Cortex B.V., had offered bets on the NBA Coach of the Year award. ZEbet had offered bets on the AFL Brownlow Medal. The KSA said offering such bets is not permitted, and both operators removed the markets.",
"The legal limit comes from the Dutch remote gambling framework under the Remote Gambling Act and the Besluit kansspelen op afstand (Remote Gambling Decree). Licensed sports betting is confined to bets on events that occur during a sporting match and on the outcome of that match. The sport must be organised and recognised by NOC*NSF or a comparable international sports body. Bets on prize elections decided by a jury, panel or vote, such as MVP or player of the year awards, are outside the licensed category. The KSA has said the same applies to political elections and player transfers.",
"Award markets are excluded because the outcome is not decided on the field. A jury or panel decision is open to influence, leaks and insider knowledge in ways that a match result is not, and it falls outside the integrity monitoring built around sporting competitions. The Dutch approach treats the permitted market list as a closed category rather than a general permission subject to exceptions. Operators that build their Dutch product from a global trading feed must therefore actively remove markets, not merely add local restrictions.",
"This is not the first time the KSA has raised the point. In March 2026 it issued a formal reminder after supervisory checks found several operators offering markets outside their licences, including award ceremony and MVP markets. That reminder said operators cannot delegate compliance to third-party sportsbook platforms or B2B suppliers and must review their products continuously. The November notice shows the KSA is checking whether that reminder has been followed and is willing to name licensees that fall short.",
"The enforcement response was measured. The KSA's notice does not refer to a fine, a binding instruction or an order subject to a penalty payment. It says the regulator wrote to the two operators, ordered them to stop and met with both. According to the KSA, both operators said no bets had been settled, attributed the offer to human error, checked their sites for other prohibited markets and are tightening internal procedures. Naming licensees publicly, however, is a sanction in itself, and repeat findings are likely to be treated more seriously, especially for operators nearing licence renewal.",
"The episode also highlights a wider compliance theme. The KSA's renewal process and its expectations on duty of care and integrity are making product governance a licensing matter, not just a trading decision. Operators should expect future renewal assessments to look at how market offerings are controlled, who signs off new markets for the Dutch domain, and how quickly non-permitted markets are identified and removed. The KSA's sports betting integrity guidance (Leidraad Integriteit Sportweddenschappen) is the obvious reference point.",
"Dutch licensees should take practical steps now. Run a full audit of every market offered on the .nl product against the permitted categories, with particular attention to outrights, specials, awards and novelty markets. Make sure supplier contracts require Dutch-specific market filtering and give the operator audit rights. Document a sign-off process for new markets and keep records showing it is followed. Where a market's status is unclear, Dutch gaming counsel can advise on the boundary before the product goes live rather than after the KSA has found it."])

add("fdj-sues-meta-illegal-online-casino-ads-france-2026",
"FDJ Sues Meta Over Illegal Casino Ads in France","Enforcement",
"FDJ United has launched summary proceedings against Meta over ads that misuse its brand to promote illegal online casinos in France.",
["netherlands","belgium","united-kingdom"],["bird-and-bird","cms","kalff-katz-and-franssen","stibbe"],"2026-11-24T11:20:00Z",[
"Licensed gambling operators are increasingly going to court against the platforms that carry illegal advertising, rather than waiting for regulators to act. FDJ United has launched summary legal proceedings against Meta in France, seeking the removal of social media adverts that, it says, misuse FDJ's brands to promote illegal online casinos. Online casino games remain prohibited in France, so any online casino offer to French players is unlawful by definition. FDJ chair and chief executive Stéphane Pallez set out the company's case in an opinion piece in Les Echos on 17 November 2026.",
"According to FDJ, the adverts used images designed to look like FDJ retail outlets and the front pages of well-known newspapers. FDJ says it reported 226 new pieces of illegal content on Meta's platforms between 18 and 21 May 2026, and that many were identical or similar to material it had flagged before. FDJ says that after discussions Meta did not give adequate assurances or put in place technical measures to identify and remove the content. Pallez said FDJ had filed a complaint over impersonation and fraudulent use of its brand through rogue social media campaigns promoting illegal gambling.",
"The choice of summary proceedings (référé) is significant. French summary procedure lets a claimant seek urgent interim measures, such as removal or blocking orders, without waiting for a full trial on the merits. A brand owner facing repeated impersonation does not need to prove every element of a damages claim to obtain an order to stop a manifestly unlawful disturbance. The case will test how far a platform can be required not only to remove content after notice but to prevent identical or similar content from reappearing.",
"That question sits at the centre of the EU's platform liability rules. Under the Digital Services Act, hosting services keep a liability exemption if they act expeditiously on notice of illegal content, and they cannot be subject to a general monitoring obligation. However, the Court of Justice of the European Union has accepted, in the Glawischnig-Piesczek line of case law, that national courts may order removal of content identical or equivalent to content already found unlawful. FDJ's emphasis on repeat material that it says is identical or similar suggests it is building its case around that distinction. Meta, as a very large online platform, is also subject to systemic risk and advertising transparency obligations under the DSA.",
"FDJ is not acting alone. In the Netherlands, the trade association VNLOK has sued Meta over illegal gambling adverts, claiming that more than 90% of gambling adverts on Facebook come from unlicensed operators and that Meta removed only a small proportion of flagged ads in May and June. Taken together, the cases suggest that licensed operators across Europe are prepared to use civil litigation, intellectual property and consumer protection law to put commercial pressure on platforms, filling gaps left by regulators whose powers over platforms are limited.",
"FDJ has also framed the case in public-policy terms. Pallez cited risks to minors, unpaid winnings, account hacking and data theft, put the number of Europeans who see illegal online gambling offers each year at more than 80 million, and cited an estimate that illegal gambling cost European governments more than €20 billion in lost tax revenue in 2025. FDJ is calling for a coalition of operators, platforms, banks, payment providers, youth-protection groups and sports bodies, and has launched an awareness campaign around the message that playing legally means playing safely.",
"For licensed operators, the practical lessons are clear. Keep systematic records of every impersonating or illegal advert found, including timestamps, screenshots and notice-and-action submissions, because that evidence is what makes a court application viable. Make sure trade mark portfolios cover the logos, retail livery and campaign assets most likely to be copied. Consider whether DSA trusted flagger status, or a coordinated approach through a trade association, would speed up removals. Specialist gaming and technology counsel can advise on the choice between regulatory complaints, summary proceedings and full litigation in each market."])

add("ecuador-sports-betting-licence-applications-decree-422-2026",
"Ecuador Sports Betting Licences: 16 Bids, Strict Rules","Market Entry",
"Ecuador is reviewing 16 sports betting licence applications under Decree 422. Fees, guarantees, AML and tax rules for operators entering.",
["colombia","peru","mexico"],["holland-and-knight-llp","greenberg-traurig-llp","bird-and-bird-llp"],"2026-11-24T12:40:00Z",[
"Ecuador's regulated sports betting market is taking shape. The Ministry of Education, Sport and Culture said on 11 November 2026 that it was reviewing 16 applications for sports betting licences. The regime, which refers to sports betting as pronósticos deportivos, rests on regulations to the Sports Law issued by President Daniel Noboa under Executive Decree 422, published on 15 June 2026. Named applicants include Ecuabet, reported to be the largest operator by revenue, Bet593, run by the Junta de Beneficencia de Guayaquil, and Forbet. The ministry has not published the full list.",
"The most consequential feature of the regime is its closed transitional window. Operators already active in Ecuador had 90 working days to apply, a period that ended on 21 October 2026. Operators that missed the deadline cannot be licensed. The regulations also bar them from getting around that exclusion by setting up new companies, changing their name, transferring assets or using third parties. For a market in which the tax authority SRI registered 65 operators in 2024, and the Superintendency of Companies counted 29 active sports betting companies as of June 2026, the deadline has reduced the field sharply.",
"The licence terms are demanding. Licences run for five years. The annual fee is 655 unified basic salaries, about USD 315,710 at the 2026 rate of USD 482, payable within the first 20 days of each licence year. Within 30 working days of notification of the licence, operators must provide a bank guarantee or insurance policy covering payments to players. For smaller domestic operators these costs are substantial, and they favour well-capitalised groups that can treat Ecuador as one part of a regional portfolio.",
"The operational requirements, set out in an October ministerial agreement, follow the pattern of other recently regulated Latin American markets. Operators must be based in Ecuador, certify their platforms and register with the financial intelligence unit, UAFE. They must verify the identity of every player, and anonymous betting is prohibited. Betting funds must be held in a dedicated Ecuadorian bank account. Applicants must show full tax compliance for the previous three years, including income tax, VAT and withholding tax on prizes. Since the decree, 15% is withheld from every prize paid, calculated per event.",
"Technical certification follows a staged approach. In the first year an international laboratory certificate is accepted. From the second year, certification must come from a laboratory authorised in Ecuador. Once the ministry issues its technical requirements, operators will have up to 90 days to comply. Operators should plan for re-certification costs and possible platform changes in year two, and should negotiate supplier contracts on that basis now.",
"The regime also addresses integrity and conflicts of interest. Sports officials with financial links to operators, and their relatives, are barred from holding a licence, which is significant in a market where club and federation sponsorship by betting brands has been common. The sports ministry is also required to set up a sector anti-money laundering unit by mid-December 2026. That unit, together with UAFE registration, will shape how source-of-funds checks, suspicious transaction reporting and player due diligence are supervised in practice.",
"For international groups, Ecuador now looks less like an entry opportunity and more like a market that will be served by the applicants already in the process, either alone or with partners. Suppliers, payment providers and B2B platform operators can still enter by contracting with licensed operators, subject to certification and AML requirements. Groups considering acquisitions or partnerships with applicants should carry out due diligence on tax history, beneficial ownership and the anti-circumvention rules, which could catch structures designed to bring excluded operators back in. Latin American gaming counsel can advise on structuring and on how the regime compares with Colombia, Peru and Mexico."])

